this takes us to the last agenda item  for the plenary session of today and the  last agenda item just before the lunch  break the ftth Awards I hand over to  Ronan to deliver the winners of 2018  ftth Awards thank you or zibethinus it  now at this point that we use this  opportunity to give a little bit of a  sneak insight with regard to some of the  findings from our updated  fiber-to-the-home panorama am as most of  you know the fibre to home council we  don't just acknowledge individuals or  operators with our awards every year we  also give a very up-to-date snapshot  with regard to how the industry is  performing in general which countries  are doing particularly well and which  operators are doing well within those  countries and this year I picked out two  snippets from the overall findings which  will be presented tomorrow morning and  they they'll be presented at the 9:30  session in conference room to the full  findings but the snippets that I'm happy  to share with you at the moment first of  all is an update on the Spanish market I  thought it was appropriate given that  we're here and if we look at where the  spanish market has managed to get to as  of September of last year so remember it  is always a little bit of a lag in our  findings here but they've now got to the  point where they've got 17 and a half  million homes passed now at this stage  which i think is a fantastic achievement  and six million subscribers as well so I  think that deserves a round of applause  for the Spanish market is really an  outstanding achievement their  penetration rate is up there at 34% now  at this point so like if I look at some  of the more mature markets out there  particularly where you've got  infrastructure based competition they're  really getting up to those very high  penetration rates now across their  customer base so it's really really  encouraging and I think what it does  show I mentioned earlier when I gave the  keynote I spoke about going from zero to  hero in such a short period of time and  we also heard from the Minister as well  with regard to putting in place the  right policies and the right regulation  to allow that to happen and I think for  all of the other European countries too  lessons to be learned in that you know  we frequently hear people complain about  how difficult it is to roll out fibre  but it just shows when the right will is  in place when the right regulatory  environment is in place and when the  right environment for investors to  invest safely is in place you can't  achieve it and you can achieve it a lot  quicker than most people think the next  one which is one that's pretty close to  my heart and it's mutually a source of  pride but also a source of embarrassment  given my role within the  fiber-to-the-home Council and that is to  announce that Ireland has finally  managed to make it onto the panorama and  when I say make it onto the panorama by  way of reminder the the gating process  to get onto the panorama is your country  first of all must be a reasonable scale  so it must have at least two hundred  thousand households and then you need at  least one percent penetration of fibre  within the country so finally Ireland  now we've got three hundred thousand  homes passed thirty thousand of those  are connected so within the last year  they've gone from pretty much zero to  about a 10 percent penetration rate slow  start there is a lot of infrastructure  based competition there I think  organizations operators like the lights  of cyro who's a joint venture with  between Vodafone and the incumbent  electricity supplier have helped  catalyze the market we've seen in  response to that the likes of the  incumbent air also get on board I think  one of the challenges that we've got is  we've got a National Broadband Plan that  unfortunately is very badly broken we've  seen the withdrawal of sciro we've seen  the withdrawal of air from the process  there only is one remaining operator  involved in that process which is e NAT  I think most people are coming to the  conclusion that ultimately we need to  start that process all over again  because we don't really have what  represents competition so that's it from  me with regard to the the sneak preview  on the panorama for tomorrow you'll get  the full insights as I mentioned  tomorrow morning conference room 2 at  9:30 that brings us on to the awards  which is a very important part and I  want to talk about first of all the  individual award and the individual  award this year I'm delighted to have  the opportunity and to highlight really  what I would refer to is one of the  leading entrepreneurs in this space  they didn't start their business in a  shiny glass building they kind of remind  me very much of hewlett-packard where  they started out of a garage where in  this scenario they happen to start their  fiber-to-the-home business out of a barn  and it wasn't their first business in  that space they've been involved in a  couple of startups as well all related  to the fiber-to-the-home so it gives me  great pleasure to be able to announce  that this year's individual winner for  the fiber-to-the-home  award is Matthew hare CEO of Giggy clear  who's Matthew here come on down here  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  thank you very much round of applause  for Matthew our next award is our  operator award and I've delighted again  once again to have the opportunity to  give out this award the company that  we've awarded this year is has been  barely out of the news over the last 12  months for all the right reasons which  is nice for a change because quite often  the the press tend to focus in on  negativity and particularly when it  comes to building out fiber it felt like  every week that this company was  announcing a new area that they were  bringing their fiber network to and even  today there's no signs of that slowing a  vibrant young dynamic company which  connectivity goes hand in hand with  their innovation connectivity to them of  course is pure fibre to the home that's  their sole focus but innovation is also  core to their culture within the  organization their wholesale networks  are now increasingly breaking new ground  in the telecom sector and I think what  they've shown to a lot of the countries  across Europe is what can be achieved if  you focus in on wholesale open access  type networks it's my great pleasure to  announce that the Italian wholesale only  provider of open fiber is this year's  winner of the fiber-to-the-home council  operator award and here to collect the  award and trill to welcome on stage  Elizabeta Reaper the CEO of open fiber  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  congratulations again thank you very  well done just a couple of quick things  before we allow everybody to go for  lunch and first of all I want to draw  your attention to something that's very  close to the heart on the  fiber-to-the-home Council and in  particular the Board of Directors as  well and that is the ongoing support for  the promotion of greater equality within  our industry and particularly within the  fiber industry greater balance within  the industry and one of the initiatives  that we started with in the  fiber-to-the-home council is quite a few  years back now at this point and we've  continued at each each and every year to  support it is the whole women and fiber  initiative many of you will have seen  that the pop-up banners dotted around  the building and historically the women  in Fiber initiative was bringing  together the women that are involved in  our industry so that they could network  and build the strength of their their  their network together but this year  what we've just like t'do in consultant  in consultation with those that are  involved in it is to open that network  up much wider so this year's women and  fiber cocktail event which is at 5:30  this evening and that will be open to  all of the members all of it sorry all  of the attendees at the conference to  attend so it highly encourage you to  show your support for this very very  important movement we're seeing a lot of  change in every single industry across  the globe  it's critically important III think it  was the lady speaker earlier from orange  mentioned a figure of 15% which is  horrendous when you think about it you  know it's a we absolutely need to do  more to get more women involved in our  industry and bringing their ideas and  their innovations to bear the other  thing I'd like to draw your attention to  is don't forget tonight is vibration  noise and vibration night is that you  know when I listen to the feedback that  comes from all of the attendees of the  conference when we go through all the  surveys there's two key things that  stand out head and shoulders above all  of the others with regard to what drives  satisfaction from attending the event  the first thing is obviously the  workshops and everybody that I spoke to  over the last sort of 24 hours that  attended the workshops yesterday  reinforced that view that they continue  to be a source of excellent knowledge  but the other next most important thing  is the networking and I actively  encourage you we're coming up on  lunchtime now go network that's what  this event is all about but this evening  at the fibration night it's the perfect  opportunity to get to meet all of your  colleagues would and peers within the  industry and learn what they're doing  and how they're doing things differently  the final piece and I think apology I  probably should have been clicking here  but it wasn't um it doesn't matter I'll  keep talking and the final piece is just  by way of a reminder for next year we're  conscious that this year there were  challenges for people to overcome to  make it to the event we're conscious  there was multiple Hopf flights for a  lot of individuals and we're very  thankful for those that went to the  effort of traveling those distances  we're conscious that for many school  holidays were on this week which also  presented the challenge there was also  Valentine's Day which I'm sure many of  us got reminded as we walked out the  door to head for the conference that we  weren't going to be around for  Valentine's Day and that always presents  a challenge so we've put a lot of effort  in for next year's conference to ensure  that we're not conflicting with holidays  or personal hallmark type holidays  either and next year's conference is  moving to Rye and Amsterdam so from a  commute perspective I don't think we can  get it any more central than that I  think the vast majority of attendees  will be able to make it on either a  single hop flight or drive to it or take  the train to it so it's the perfect  location so we're really looking forward  to probably a record-breaking attendance  next year at the Rye event so with that  I'll wrap things up and let everybody go  for lunch Bon Appetit  you
 

